 for us to look at them  and one convenient way to do that is to  use cloud publish  so if we want to take the manipulate  from before and  publish it to the web we can just say  cloud publish and then give it a name  and that will return a cloud object  cloud object is really just a reference  to a file in the wolfram cloud  in this case it's in my account with the  given name  and if i open that it opens this in a  so-called deployed view where i can  again interact with it  just one thing to keep in mind is that  there's different views of cloud objects  or different url types  most of the time you'll want to share  the so-called object view  denoted by this obj in the url with  other people because that's what's  publicly available if you set the  permissions accordingly  so yeah that's that's what you usually  use to share notebooks with other people  but sometimes it's useful to share url  that allows editing of the notebook  and that's what the the so-called  environment view is about  you can construct a url of that type  with the cloud object option cloud  object url type  and if i click this link now it opens  in this environment view where i can  also  edit this and you know have a file menu  and just more things i can do to work  with this notebook  something we just added recently is  download url type  which is a convenient way to hand out  the url that immediately triggers a  download  of the notebook so if i click this or  just  download the notebook to my local file  system  so that's just a general overview of how  notebooks work in the cloud there's also  other things so like  forms here we're publishing a so-called  form function  that takes a website name  as a parameter and then immediately  redirects  to that website so i can type something  like wikipedia  and it will go to wikipedia.org  you can see here let's try something  else so this is  a very simplistic search engine  implemented in a few lines of language  code  and of course you can add many more  fields  of different types and make use of a lot  of the  natural language understanding built  into the wolfram language  to to deal with forms  if your audience are not humans directly  but more like other programs  there is a way to deploy apis to the  cloud through a construct called aki  function  it works pretty similarly to form  function  just that it's not not meant for  human consumption but usually to be  called by  by other programs so the input will  usually be  some json payload and then you get back  another json data structure  in this case we're deploying  a very useful api that tells us whether  the year 2020 is finally over yet but  unfortunately  the answer is no still not there yet 88  days remaining so still gotta hang in  there for a while  um we can't change that but  we can cheat a little bit and in this  case pass in a different  date and on january 1st 2021  it will finally be there um  again this has the full power of  the natural language understanding of  the world from language building so i  can also  pass in things like yesterday here and  it will  interpret it as the right date and give  the right result obviously  so that's a few of the things you can  deploy to the cloud  just a few more notes about how cloud  objects work in general  we've seen this function cloud publish  which is essentially just cloud deploy  with public permissions and a few other  tweaks but they're more or less  equivalent  there's many other functions to manage  content in the cloud like copy file  which works both to and from a cloud  object handle  you can use cloud export to  export stuff to the cloud there's a  function cloud objects  to enumerate cloud objects in your cloud  account  and many more there's a whole guide page  in the documentation  that lists various functions that you  can take a look at  right so that's it in terms of cloud  objects now i want to talk about a few  of the things we've been working on  recently  one thing that just came to the cloud  over the last year  is rule from alpha notebook edition  which  basically combines the simplicity of  wolfram alpha  with the whole power of mathematica and  what you can do in the world from  language  there is a whole separate product that  you can subscribe to separately  to get access to go from alpha notebooks  but it's also available from most of our  other products  and in this case i can just go to the  file menu and say new  rule from alpha mode notebook and it  will create  this type of notebook where instead of  typical input cells where i type code i  can now type in  from alpha queries in natural english  language  so i type in what  city am i in and press  enter i will  get the result eventually so i'm in  vienna right now  i can keep exploring that result through  the suggestions bar which is  particularly handy  for notebooks like this you could ask  for the population or  where it is i could also  make another query and ask what's the  population of vienna over time  and notice that it also gives me  a corresponding rule from language input  in this case  it makes use of entities in the language  and then a time series as the result if  you want to look at that i could just  say  offstat and  what's what you can see here what's  really useful about rule from alpha  notebooks  is that they have some state in them so  you can refer  to the previous cell using that  and it will understand that you know i  want to plot the previous result  so i think this is a very useful way to  get familiar with the wolfram language  even if you're not used to programming  yet  you can just type regular english  language and get back results and at the  same time also see  what the corresponding wolfram language  commands would be  and learn the language slowly that way  so yeah it's nice that we have full from  alpha notebook edition on the cloud now  but new features aside the priority for  us recently has certainly been  improving robustness and performance of  the cloud  and one area that  i want to highlight in particular is  improved kernel isolation  we're now able to use a fresh new kernel  process  for each invocation of an api function  or other deployed objects in the cloud  we used to be not able to do that  because it was just too costly to start  a new  kernel process for each http request  but we're now using process forking at  the system level  to basically get a fresh process  in a pretty cheap way and  that's mostly invisible to users  but under the hood it's it's a big boost  for robustness of the whole system  and also if you're running a private  cloud you might be interested  in enabling this continuing in terms of  performance and robustness just on the  other end of the stack  is our project for client-side graphics  rendering  for most graphics what we still need to  do is  generate some extra data on the server  side so that the client can  turn it into svg graphics but what we're  increasingly trying to do  is render graphics purely on the client  side in our javascript code  this started with basically rendering  disk and circle boxes  and simple rgb colors but we're  constantly adding  more graphics directives and other  graphics features  to this client-side implementation  so what this allows us to do is to  render certain graphics  purely on the client side without the  extra complication and overhead of going  back to the server  for extra data and in combination with  a client-side evaluator that we have  that can evaluate simple workflow  language expressions purely on the  client side  we can create some pretty cool stuff  like this  animation here of of planets  that is running purely on the client  side without  needing any kernel but it's actually  implemented  in pure wolfram language  and yeah as we as we progress and add  more and more features to this  client-side graphics implementation  this will just be more powerful  another project in the area of graphics  is what we call interactive graphics  where the idea is that for certain plots  and  other graphics we add various  interactive effects  to make them nicer to look at and to  work with  so here we have a plot with points and  the lines and  what we might do is highlight the  nearest point  and also add drop lines so that you can  easily see  what the corresponding point on the axis  is  and this is the combined effort  between annotations added by the kernel  to the graphics box itself and  corresponding implementation on the  cloud side  so some of this is hopefully making it  into 12  2 and then we'll work in the cloud  and morris is probably coming later  including  exposing some lower level mechanisms to  users to also add  their their annotations and effects if  they wanted to  so there's a lot going on in terms of  graphics rendering in the cloud  but even more generally than that we're  always trying to  improve notebook performance in the  cloud  and particularly loading speed of  notebooks  one thing we've had for a while is  server-side rendering of notebooks  that generate some static html on the  server so that the page can load faster  and you see something pretty much right  away well  stuff still renders interactively in the  background  and and then everything is interactive  but  especially for very large notebooks that  could still  take quite a long time and it was kind  of distracting  to see the the notebook taking  a long time to become interactive so  what we've just been working on recently  is  to hook up even faster  static rendering mode and give you an  idea of that  let me show you a notebook how it might  have looked like before  if you load it in the cloud so this is a  pretty large notebook  and you can see it it takes a while  until anything shows up there's the blue  progress bar at the top  and then it it slowly makes its way  through  until it has it has finally rendered  all the cells on the client side and  for such a large notebook there isn't a  whole lot we can do  to to actually speed up uh  you know the rendering of each  individual cell much more  it just takes some time to do that in a  browser  but what we can do is it's trying to  avoid that live rendering  on the client side altogether and just  keep using  the static rendering that you already  have from the server  to to show the notebook and only start  the live rendering  once it's actually necessary and  that's exactly what this so-called  static rendering  method is about so on  this new branch that we have with this  new implementation  here's how this would look like first of  all  you can see the the static html cache  uh coming in right away and then it's  just much much faster  to to render the whole notebook  and now i can already scroll through it  might take a little bit  for each uh sort of section of the  notebook to render that's just the way  the browser works but it's it's pretty  smooth and i can just  scroll through it read the whole  notebook and in this case there isn't  even much missing  even though it's it's still using the  static rendering from the server  there isn't a whole lot of interactivity  in this notebook anyway  so some of the work that would have been  done  wouldn't really be necessary anyway for  other notebooks  that are more interactive here's what  will happen  so if in this case load a notebook  with the static rendering mode enabled  and  i have here manipulate so that really  wants to be interactive  even though we didn't render it  interactively right away  but then what happens is as soon as i  start interacting with that cell and  click here  it renders that one cell and makes it  interactive and i can just  keep dragging the slider and it  basically just works  the same let's say for for buttons you  can just press them and they they become  interactive  also graphics that you can resize and so  on so  it's it's still a pretty smooth  experience  but but just delaying a bunch of the  work that would happen  right away when you load the notebook  delaying that until later when it's  actually necessary  which makes for a much faster loading  speed especially for large notebooks  we'll probably enable this as sort of a  heuristic  for large notebooks beyond a certain  size  but there's also the cloud rendering  method option  that you can use to control this  explicitly  and this is coming in cloud 157 so in  the in the  upcoming cloud release  the goal with all of this is to be able  to use cloud notebooks everywhere  and to publish any content you like to  the cloud  but also to embed notebooks in other  websites that you might have  and one thing that particularly helps  with this is the wolfram notebook and  better  library that we released last year and  are continuously  improving as well so this is a  javascript library  that you can feed any url of a public  cloud notebook  and it will embed that notebook in  any other page some example applications  that already make use of this is for  instance  wolfram community which is very nice so  that you can  just integrate notebooks into community  posts  there's also the notebook archive coming  up with  tons and tons of notebooks based on the  cloud  and then there's also for instance the  github from document viewer  that allows you to take any repository  on github that might contain notebooks  and view them in the cloud so that's  just a few examples  and there's certainly many more use  cases  for for notebooks so these are a few  things we've been working on recently of  course there's  much more going on in addition to that  for instance we're working on  performance improvements  in terms of using the wolfram language  compiler to generate web assembly  that can be executed on the client side  without needing a kernel  we're also working on a new  implementation of cloud expression  which is a convenient way to persist  expressions in the cloud  we're starting to work on accessibility  and improving the way cloud notebooks  interoperate with screen readers for  instance  there's also a big project to move the  cloud to containers and kubernetes  which i think will be particularly  interesting for private clouds  and scaling them and having a more  redundant architecture there  and there is also still the big project  of implementing a full type setting  editor  in cloud notebooks what we have so far  is  basically just a one-dimensional code  editor and of course there can be type  setting  in the output but it's not editable yet  and that there will be a longer term  project to make that work  if you want to know all the details of  what's going on  you can take a look at our release notes  with every cloud release we also  publish a notebook that gives all the  details what happened in that release  i'm also happy that we finally have a  fairly accurate and complete list  of supported and unsupported wolfram  language functions  in the cloud so you can take a look at  that document  to see what's expected to work and  what's not expected to work yet  in the cloud and if you want to know  more details about cloud notebooks in  particular  you can take a look at these uh  implementation notes  that have a lot of detail about  various aspects of cloud notebooks you  want to get in touch please don't  hesitate to  shoot me an email or get in touch on  wolfram community  i'm always uh looking forward to  learning more about  what you do with the cloud so yep that's  it for my site  and thanks for listening

